<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Equinoctial France</h1><p class="st-meta">1612 CE – 1615 CE · capital Saint-Louis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived French colony on the island of Maranhão whose fort, Saint-Louis, survives as the city of São Luís in northern Brazil.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1612 an expedition led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daniel-de-la-touche">Daniel de La Touche</a>, seigneur de La Ravardière, and François de Razilly, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capuchins">Capuchin</a> friars, landed on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maranhao">Maranhão</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil's</a> northern coast. They built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-luis-maranhao">Fort Saint-Louis</a>, named after King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiii-of-france">Louis XIII</a>, and allied with local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupinamba">Tupinambá</a> groups.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The colony was a small trading and missionary outpost. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capuchins">Capuchins</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-dabbeville">Claude d'Abbeville</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yves-devreux">Yves d'Évreux</a> wrote detailed accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupinamba">Tupinambá</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbeville">Abbeville</a> took several Tupinambá to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where they were baptised before the court in 1613. French support from home was weak, and the settlers relied on indigenous allies for food and protection.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Portugal, then united with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, sent forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeronimo-de-albuquerque-maranhao">Jerónimo de Albuquerque</a>, who defeated the French at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-guaxenduba">Guaxenduba</a> in 1614. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daniel-de-la-touche">La Ravardière</a> surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-luis-maranhao">Saint-Louis</a> in 1615. The Portuguese kept the town as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-luis">São Luís</a>, later capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-maranhao">State of Maranhão</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/equinoctial-france.jpg" alt="Equinoctial France" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Oren neu dag (talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_flag_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1612 CE – 1615 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saint-Louis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Daniel de La Touche</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>France Équinoxiale</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1614&amp;focus=equinoctial-france" aria-label="Equinoctial France on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3450 2272 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Equinoctial France lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3770 2528L5054 728M3770 2528L3944 2917"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M3944 2917h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3770 2528h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3770 2528h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1614&amp;focus=equinoctial-france">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

